,92,47822,17,18,25547,17,19,13333,10699,2359,2075,47822,17,19,13333,92,10,87,47822,17,19,28735,11035,1291,568,14085,10061,400,89,62,16,11,1147,62,17,11,1124,507,2412,11,1147,1710,3,387,279,12460,17516,315,400,47,2075,15087,11,323,1077,400,89,4698,61,17,24239,4698,35093,15159,74,92,72,3,369,400,74,28,16,11,220,17,11,1124,507,2412,11,435,54876,1380,400,72,34433,26888,19999,16,31716,11,323,400,64,4698,3,323,400,65,4698,3,525,1931,5109,13,7379,198,59,26056,1242,15159,74,28,16,92,47822,81,52398,65,4698,91,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $$P(x)=24x^{24}+\\sum_{j=1}^{23}(24-j)\\left(x^{24-j}+x^{24+j}\\right).$$Let $z_1, z_2, \\ldots, z_r$ be the distinct zeros of $P(x)$, and let $z_k^2=a_k+b_{k}i$ for $k=1, 2, \\ldots, r$, where $i=\\sqrt{-1}$, and $a_k$ and $b_k$ are real numbers. Find\n\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{r}|b_k|.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6e59f3abed445e65fd2d8bc0a6d3555b1672293de030c3fa08c20b4900627ecf"},"seconds":51.07715106010437,"seed":797054219038720664,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the given problem, we start by analyzing the polynomial \\( P(x) \\):\n\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( x^{24-j} + x^{24+j} \\right).\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the polynomial in a more convenient form:\n\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24-j} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24+j}.\n\\]\n\nNotice that the second sum can be rewritten by changing the index \\( j \\) to \\( 24-j \\):\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24+j} = \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-(24-j)) x^{24+(24-j)} = \\sum_{j=1}^{23} j x^{48-j}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the polynomial becomes:\n\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24-j} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} j x^{48-j}.\n\\]\n\nWe can combine the sums:\n\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24-j} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} j x^{48-j}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we observe that \\( P(x) \\) is a polynomial of degree 48. To find the zeros of \\( P(x) \\), we consider the symmetry and properties of the polynomial. Notice that \\( P(x) \\) is symmetric about \\( x = 0 \\), meaning if \\( z \\) is a root, then \\( -z \\) is also a root.\n\nWe can use the fact that the polynomial \\( P(x) \\) can be expressed in terms of its roots. Let \\( z_1, z_2, \\ldots, z_{48} \\) be the roots of \\( P(x) \\). Since the polynomial is symmetric, the roots come in conjugate pairs, and the polynomial can be written as:\n\n\\[\nP(x) = 24 \\prod_{k=1}^{24} (x - z_k)(x - \\overline{z_k}).\n\\]\n\nGiven \\( z_k^2 = a_k + b_k i \\), we need to find \\( \\sum_{k=1}^{r} |b_k| \\).\n\nSince the polynomial is symmetric and the coefficients are real, the imaginary parts of the roots must cancel out in pairs. Therefore, for each pair \\( z_k \\) and \\( \\overline{z_k} \\), the imaginary part \\( b_k \\) and \\( -b_k \\) will contribute \\( |b_k| + |-b_k| = 2|b_k| \\).\n\nGiven that there are 24 such pairs, the total sum of the absolute values of the imaginary parts is:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{48} |b_k| = 2 \\sum_{k=1}^{24} |b_k|.\n\\]\n\nHowever, since the polynomial is symmetric and the roots are paired, the sum of the absolute values of the imaginary parts of the distinct roots is:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{24} |b_k| = 12.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{12}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
